The quadratic equation \(x^{2}-3x+2=0\) has two real roots:

\[
x=\frac{\,3 \pm \sqrt{\,(-3)^2-4(1)(2)\,}}{2}
   =\frac{\,3 \pm \sqrt{1}\,}{2}
   =\begin{cases}
      \dfrac{3+1}{2}=2\\[4pt]
      \dfrac{3-1}{2}=1
     \end{cases}
\]

So the roots are **\(x_1 = 1\)** and **\(x_2 = 2\)**.
